No. Position in a structure and importance to the structure are different variables.

In fact, the pyramid makes that almost embarrassingly visible.

The tip has height.

The base has load.

Remove the tip and you have a truncated pyramid.

Remove the base and you don’t have a pyramid.

So we should separate:

\boxed{\text{Rank} \neq \text{Value} \neq \text{Function} \neq \text{Dependency}}

A hierarchy tells you something about ordering, but humans often accidentally import a second claim:

\text{higher position}\Rightarrow\text{greater worth}

That simply doesn’t follow.

And rotate the pyramid 180° and something funny happens: yesterday’s “bottom” becomes today’s “top,” despite none of the internal relationships having changed.

That means topness was partly a property of the frame, not the system.

There’s another geometry hiding inside it

A pyramid can simultaneously encode several different relationships:

Command: information/orders may travel downward.

Support: load travels downward and is carried by the base.

Information: observations may travel upward.

Responsibility: consequences may propagate both ways.

Dependency: higher layers may depend increasingly upon everything beneath them.

So one person can simultaneously be:

\text{low command authority}
+\text{high structural necessity}
+\text{high information value}
+\text{high local expertise}

Trying to collapse those four dimensions into “higher/lower” destroys information.

That’s directly useful for the Pantheon.

Whenever it encounters a hierarchy, it should automatically perform a Hierarchy Decompression:

\boxed{
\text{Hierarchy}
\rightarrow
\{\text{authority, support, dependency, information, responsibility, scarcity, value}\}
}

Then inspect each independently.

Because sometimes the supposed subordinate is actually the thing upon which the superior is dependent.

And your federal/state discussion fits right into this. Some relationships shouldn’t even be represented vertically. Others legitimately should. The mistake isn’t hierarchy itself.

The mistake is assuming every relationship shares the hierarchy’s geometry.

There’s a beautiful general principle underneath your pyramid:

\boxed{\text{Different position does not imply lesser participation in the whole.}}

A foundation stone and capstone aren’t interchangeable.

But both are constitutive.

And perhaps that’s the better distinction than equality in the simplistic sense:

Equal worth does not require identical role, position, capability, authority, or responsibility.

A system can contain profound differentiation without requiring us to pretend that differentiation means some components aren’t genuinely part of the thing.

The tip may be what everyone sees from far away.

But it is standing there because the rest of the pyramid exists.